exports[`game asset designer handoff template snapshots > 'duo' 'game-asset-designer' → 'planner' renders the complete contract 1`] = `
"> ⚠️ **Template notice — not part of the message. Do not copy this notice into your handoff.**
> Recipient visibility: the \`planner\` agent receives only the text inside your \`handoff --next-role="planner"\` command. It cannot see anything you write in this agent session, or tool output. Put your complete deliverable in the handoff message.

Handoff template \`game-asset-designer\` → \`planner\` (copy only the body sections below; do not copy this line, the notice, or the instructions after the body):

Instructions (not part of the message): Return a complete asset delivery report for the request the planner named. State only what you established. Label every checked property with one evidence label: \`measured\`, \`inspected\`, \`specified\`, \`unverified\`. Never claim a polycount, texel density, loudness, color space, or visual-quality result without \`measured\` or \`inspected\` evidence. Numeric results (polycount, texel density, resolution, loudness, true peak, sample rate, bit depth) and color-space slot checks require \`measured\`; \`inspected\` covers only visual or audible judgments. A budget or target you write into the spec is a requirement and is labelled \`specified\`, not a result. Do not edit source code or write outside the named output location. Use the exact sections below, in this order.

## Summary
<one to three sentences: what was delivered, whether it is spec only or includes files, and the count of failed or unverified checks>

## Target and assumptions
<engine, version, platform tier, style reference used; each assumption you made and why>

## Asset manifest
<one entry per asset: name, category, file format, path (or \`Specified only.\`), dimensions/units/scale, pivot, naming, LOD list with triangle budgets, texture set with resolution, color space and channel packing, atlas/pixel grid/frame data, audio format and loudness target, import settings>

## Quality checklist results
<per asset: each applicable checklist item as \`pass\`, \`fail\`, or \`n/a\`, with its evidence label and, for \`measured\`, the command and value; write exactly \`None failed.\` after the list if no item failed>

## Style consistency
<how the set matches the style reference; deviations; what was compared and how>

## Files created
<repository-relative paths and a one-line description each; write exactly \`None.\` for spec only>

## Sources
<URLs for engine or platform rules relied on, with version or date checked>

## Open questions
<clarifications needed from the planner, each with a recommended default; write exactly \`None.\` if none>

## Not verified
<every property you could not measure or inspect; write exactly \`None.\` if none>

Instructions after the body (not part of the message): run the normal handoff command to \`planner\` with the complete body above, then stop.

\`\`\`bash
chatroom handoff --chatroom-id="<chatroom-id>" --role="game-asset-designer" --next-role="planner" << 'CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END'
---MESSAGE---
[Your message here]
CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END
\`\`\`"
`;

exports[`game asset designer handoff template snapshots > 'duo' 'planner' → 'game-asset-designer' renders the complete contract 1`] = `
"> ⚠️ **Template notice — not part of the message. Do not copy this notice into your handoff.**
> Recipient visibility: the \`game-asset-designer\` agent receives only the text inside your \`handoff --next-role="game-asset-designer"\` command. It cannot see anything you write in this agent session, or tool output. Put your complete deliverable in the handoff message.

Handoff template \`planner\` → \`game-asset-designer\` (copy only the body sections below; do not copy this line, the notice, or the instructions after the body):

Instructions (not part of the message): Pass the asset request as a brief the designer can act on without guessing. Provide every field below; write \`Not specified.\` for a field you cannot fill and the designer will ask or apply a stated default. Ask for a specification and report only unless you explicitly request generation.

## Asset request
<one to three sentences: what the assets are for and the user-visible goal>

## Target
<engine and version; genre and camera; platform tier (mobile, PC, console, VR); render pipeline if known>

## Assets requested
<numbered list; each item: asset name, category (3D model, PBR textures, 2D sprite/UI, audio, other), purpose, quantity or variants>

## Budgets and conventions
<triangle budget per LOD, texel density or pixel grid, texture resolution and atlas limits, loudness target, naming convention, folder layout; or \`Not specified.\`>

## Style reference
<repository paths, URLs, or a written style sheet; or \`Not specified.\`>

## Deliverable mode
<\`spec only\` or \`spec and generate\`; if generating, the allowed tools and the exact output directory>

## Acceptance criteria
<what must be true for the planner to accept the delivery>

Instructions after the body (not part of the message): Use this template only under the conditions below; the body above ends at \`## Acceptance criteria\`.

### When to use the game asset designer
- the request creates or changes game art or audio assets (models, textures, sprites, UI art, sound effects);
- assets must meet engine import conventions or budgets before a builder integrates them;
- the user asks for an asset spec, style sheet, or asset audit.

Skip it for code-only work or for a trivial asset rename.

After completing the work, the game asset designer must return its delivery report to \`planner\` with the normal handoff command.

\`\`\`bash
chatroom handoff --chatroom-id="<chatroom-id>" --role="planner" --next-role="game-asset-designer" << 'CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END'
---MESSAGE---
[Your message here]
CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END
\`\`\`"
`;
